+## 33. Nester/Team-Leader/Ordering My-Work Queues, BOM Moved Off Batch Onto Room, and a New Ordering Role
+
+A product-owner request to make three roles' work show up where they actually look for it, instead of requiring a trip through Notifications or a project's Batches tab: a room whose IFC has been issued should show up on the **Nester's** My Work so they can batch (or nest solo) and progress it; a room submitted for internal review should show up on the **Team Leader's** My Work, with an inline approve/feedback action instead of hunting for it; the Team Leader "is also doing detailer sometimes," so they should be plannable in Planning like a Detailer; and a new **Ordering** role should see, on their own My Work, every job whose BOM is ready — that's the entirety of what they do.
+
+The first pass at this assumed BOM drafting was still a step *within* the Nester's own Batch lifecycle (§21.4's `bom_pending → bom_review → nesting → complete`), since that's where the existing code already put it. Checked with the product owner rather than assumed, and corrected: **BOM is drafted by a Detailer, per room, before a Nester ever sees it** — "nester's job is just nesting." That single correction reshaped the whole feature: BOM moved entirely off `Batch` and onto `Room` as its own `WorkflowStage`, and the `Batch` lifecycle simplified down to just `pending → nesting → complete`.
+
+**BOM Drafted, a new Room stage.** `DEFAULT_WORKFLOW_STAGES` (`app/models/workflow_stage.py`) gains `bom_drafted` (sequence 9, between `ifc_issued` and the now-10th `complete`) — the Detailer's own single click once IFC is issued, mirrored in `_STAGE_TRANSITION_RULES` (`app/services/room_service.py`) as `("ifc_issued", "bom_drafted"): (UserRole.DETAILER,)`, landing on `RoomWorkflowStatus.WAITING` (nothing to review — the room is just waiting on a Nester next). The pre-existing `ifc_issued → complete` direct shortcut (for rooms needing no BOM/nesting at all) is intentionally left in place alongside this. Migration `f6bb7a01a08e_add_bom_drafted_stage` inserts the stage and renumbers `complete` using the same two-pass offset-then-real-value trick `a1f3c9d2e6b7` established, then recomputes every room's `progress` against the new 10-stage total.
+
+**Batch, simplified.** `BatchStatus` (`app/models/enums.py`) drops `BOM_PENDING`/`BOM_REVIEW` — with BOM gone from Batch entirely, there's nothing left for a Team Leader to review at the batch level, so the old review gate goes with it. `Batch.bom_started_at` is removed; `nesting_started_at` stays. `assert_rooms_batch_eligible` (`app/services/batch_service.py`) now gates on `bom_drafted` (`sequence >= bom_drafted.sequence`, excluding `ifc_revision`/`complete` — same reasoning shape as before, just re-pointed) instead of `ifc_issued`. `POST /batches/{id}/start-bom` is gone; `POST /batches/{id}/start-nesting` now performs the `pending → nesting` transition itself (not just the timestamp, since there's no longer a separate reviewer-driven move into `nesting` to wait for) — `_ALLOWED_TRANSITIONS` is now just `{PENDING: {NESTING}, NESTING: {COMPLETE}}`. Migration `97b8f6ebe1ba_simplify_batch_status` rebuilds the Postgres `batch_status` enum from scratch (Postgres can't drop enum values in place), remapping any existing `BOM_PENDING`/`BOM_REVIEW` row to `PENDING` (both fold to the same place — either way there's nothing left to review), and drops `bom_started_at`.
+
+**Three new queues, one new eligible-rooms query each** (`app/services/batch_service.py`, `app/services/room_service.py`):
+
+- `list_batch_eligible_rooms` — unbatched rooms sitting exactly at `bom_drafted`. No per-room "assigned nester" field exists, so — same "open shared pool" shape as `plan_service.list_eligible_rooms` — this is the same list for every Nester. `GET /rooms/batch-eligible`.
+- `list_review_queue_rooms` — rooms at `ifa_internal_review`/`ifc_internal_review`. A Manager or Admin sees every one, unscoped (matching `has_admin_bypass`'s "sees everything" precedent); a Team Leader sees only their own projects' (`Project.team_leader_id == actor.id`, matching exactly who `_notify_team_leader_of_review_request`, §29, actually notifies). `GET /rooms/needs-review` — any other role gets `[]` rather than a 403, since the frontend only ever calls it for the roles it applies to and an empty list is still the honest answer.
+- `list_ready_to_order_rooms` — rooms at `bom_drafted` with `Room.materials_ordered_at IS NULL`. Deliberately independent of batching: ordering material only needs the BOM to exist, not for a Nester to have grouped the room into a Batch yet. `GET /rooms/ready-to-order`.
+
+**A new Ordering role.** `UserRole.ORDERING` (`app/models/enums.py`), added via the standard `ALTER TYPE ... ADD VALUE` autocommit-block migration (same pattern as every prior enum-value addition in this codebase). A new nullable `Room.materials_ordered_at` timestamp, set once by `POST /rooms/{id}/mark-materials-ordered` (`require_ordering`, mirroring `require_nester`'s shape) — same "informational, set-once, harmless to call twice" pattern as `Batch.nesting_started_at`, not tied to `workflow_stage` since ordering happens in parallel with (or after) nesting, not as a pipeline stage of its own. Migration `7c9f83dc5e9b_add_ordering_role_and_materials_ordered_at`.
+
+**A Nester's own batches, listable.** Batches were previously only ever listed per-project (`GET /projects/{id}/batches`); since a Nester's batch-eligible room pool on My Work already spans every project, their in-progress batches needed to too. `GET /batches/mine` (`require_nester`) returns every non-`complete` batch with `nester_id == current_user.id`, across all projects, newest-updated first.
+
+**Team Leader, plannable for Room tasks.** `_assert_task_matches_user_role` (`app/api/routes/planning.py`) now accepts `UserRole.TEAM_LEADER` alongside `UserRole.DETAILER` for a Room task (a Batch task stays Nester-only, unchanged) — mirrored on the frontend by adding `"team_leader"` to `PLANNABLE_ROLES` (`app/(app)/planning/page.tsx`). No further backend change was needed for a Team Leader to then actually *act* as a detailer on that room — `has_admin_bypass` (§26) already lets Team Leader (and Admin) through every entry in `_STAGE_TRANSITION_RULES` regardless of role. Planning a Room task onto a Team Leader sets `Room.assigned_detailer_id` to them the same as for any Detailer (`plan_service.py`), which is what makes the room show up on their own `GET /rooms/mine` afterward.
+
+**Frontend — My Work rebuilt as role-aware queues** (`app/(app)/my-work/page.tsx`), instead of the one bucketed "your assigned rooms" list it used to be exclusively:
+
+- **Detailer** (and a Team Leader on any room actually assigned to them via Planning) — the original bucketed queue, unchanged in behaviour.
+- **Nester** — batch-eligible rooms grouped by project with checkboxes ("Nest this room" for one selection, "Batch N rooms" for several — the same `POST /projects/{id}/batches` either way), plus a "Your batches" list (`GET /batches/mine`) with inline Start Nesting/Nesting Complete actions.
+- **Team Leader / Manager / Admin** — the review queue (`GET /rooms/needs-review`), each room rendered with `ReviewGateCard` — pulled out of the room detail page into its own shared file, `features/rooms/ReviewGateCard.tsx` (§32 built it inline in `app/(app)/rooms/[id]/page.tsx`; it now needs to render in a list on My Work too, so a page-local function no longer fit). A Team Leader also sees their own Detailer-style queue below it, but only once they actually have an assigned room — showing an empty "nothing assigned" card under the review queue every single time would be noise for a job most Team Leaders aren't doing most days; a plain Detailer always sees their queue, since it's their whole page.
+- **Ordering** — the ready-to-order list (`GET /rooms/ready-to-order`) with a "Mark Materials Ordered" button per row.
+
+The room detail page's own "Your actions" card (`isDetailer` in `app/(app)/rooms/[id]/page.tsx`) now also renders for a Team Leader, but *only* on a room actually assigned to them (`room.assigned_detailer?.id === user.id`) — not on every room they open. A blanket role check there would have hidden the generic `StageTransitionCard` (their reviewer picker for *other* rooms' internal-review/client-outcome gates) behind the simpler detailer card everywhere, which would have quietly broken their existing review-approval workflow on any room not their own.
+
+**A Detailer's own BOM click.** `ifc_issued` moved out of `lib/room-workflow.ts`'s `WAITING_ON_SOMEONE_ELSE` set (there's live detailer work there again now — drafting the BOM) and `bom_drafted` moved in (once drafted, the room is waiting on a Nester, same as any other entry in that set). `RoomActions.tsx` gained "Start BOM" (`startLabel`, for the `ifc_issued` stage specifically) and "BOM Complete" (`nextStageLabel`, for a `bom_drafted` target) — reusing the exact same generic Start/Next-Stage machinery every other stage already uses, not a bespoke button; "BOM Complete" is safe to name directly (unlike the detailer's IFA/IFC submissions, §32) since nobody reviews the BOM afterward.
+
+Verified live against the real seeded database, working the new pipeline end to end in one pass: a Detailer clicks "BOM Complete" on a room at `ifc_issued` (200, room now shows on `GET /rooms/batch-eligible`); a Nester creates a batch from it (`POST /projects/{id}/batches`, 201) and calls `start-nesting` (200, confirmed it performs both the `pending → nesting` move and sets `nesting_started_at` in the one call) then the generic status-transition to `complete` (200, room cascades to `workflow_stage.key == "complete"` with `progress == 100`); `GET /batches/mine` correctly returns a Nester's two seeded non-complete batches across two different projects and excludes the seeded completed one. Role edges: a Detailer gets 403 creating a batch; a Nester gets 403 marking materials ordered; a Team Leader gets 200 being assigned a Room-type Planning task but still 400 for a Batch-type one (Nester-only, unchanged). `GET /rooms/needs-review`, `/rooms/ready-to-order`, `/rooms/batch-eligible` each returned exactly the expected rooms for their role; marking a room's materials ordered correctly removed it from the Ordering role's queue. On the frontend: `npx tsc --noEmit` and `next build` both pass clean across the whole app after the `UserRole`/`BatchStatus`/`Room`/`Batch`/`WorkflowStageKey` type changes (several `Record<UserRole, ...>`/`Record<BatchStatus, ...>` maps in `lib/status.ts` needed updating, exactly as TypeScript's exhaustiveness checking was relied on to surface).
